  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 236 AM EDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nCurrent-Thursday...With a frontal boundary in proximity, we will \nsee occasional onshore moving showers/ISOLD storms this morning. \nThe boundary will begin to lift back northward today as weak Atlc \nhigh pressure ridging pushes back toward the east central FL coast\non Thu. The weak pressure pattern will permit early development \nand advancement well inland of the ECSB this afternoon and perhaps\nnot quite as far into the interior for Thu. Nonetheless, an\neventual late day/evening sea breeze collision will occur across\nthe interior both days. PWATs remain very high, well in excess of\n2\". Storm steering will be light and toward the NE/N today \nshifting more to light SW on Thu. Overall storm motion will be \nweak and erratic at times with stronger boundary collisions. PoPs \ntoday 40-60% with highest values favoring the interior this\nafternoon and 50-70% areawide on Thu. Aloft, weak troughing \ncontinues across the central peninsula enhancing convection. \nPrimary storm impacts remain frequent lightning strikes, gusty \nwinds locally 40-50 mph, and torrential downpours capable of 1-3\"\nin a relatively short period (esp interior for today) promoting \nminor/nuisance flooding in repeated rounds or across locations \nthat may have recently seen abundant rainfall. \n\nMax temps near climo in the U80s/L90s. Humid overnight with mins in \nthe 70s. Peak heat indices in the U90s to L-M100s ahead of daily \nstorms. \n\nFri-Tue...Aloft weak troughing will aid diurnal convection into Sat, \nthen mid-level high pressure begins to build/strengthen across the \nDeep South into early next week. At the surface, initial high \npressure ridging near the central FL peninsula will move slowly \nnorthward into this weekend, though we still retain a fairly weak \npressure gradient across the coverage warning area. A deeper \nonshore flow pattern will develop late Sun into early next week. \nDeep moisture will continue to surge across the area as we return \nto a more typical sea breeze and diurnal convection pattern. \nFairly high PoP numbers (60-80%) for Fri-Sun, then perhaps a \nslight drop-off into Mon/Tue (40-60%). Primary storm impacts \ncontinue to revolve around locally heavy rainfall. Near seasonal \ntemps for highs (U80s-L90s) with lows in the 70s and conditions \nremaining humid. Peak heat indices continue in the U90s to M100s.",
